%%% % @format
-module(libdecaf).
-moduledoc """
Hash messages, authenticate data, sign messages, and agree on shared secrets with libdecaf.

Use `spongerng_random_bytes/1` to generate random bytes without managing a PRNG
state. Native threads seed and periodically reseed their private SpongeRNG from
OS entropy, falling back to `crypto:strong_rand_bytes/1` when necessary.

The **EdDSA API** selects signatures through `{Algorithm, Key}` or
`{Algorithm, Key, Context}` parameters. `eddsa_keypair/1,2` returns an opaque
native keypair; extraction returns the public key and original secret seed.
Signing also accepts seeds, encoded `Seed || PublicKey` signing keys, and
public/secret pairs from the curve modules. Ed25519ph and Ed448ph support native
streaming through the `eddsa_sign_*` and `eddsa_verify_*` functions. Pass original
message bytes; the prehash is computed internally. Contexts belong to each operation.

The **ECDH API** generates X25519 or X448 keypairs with `ecdh_keypair/1,2`.
`ecdh_shared_secret/3` takes the algorithm, local secret, and peer public key;
either key argument may instead be a keypair. It returns raw shared-secret bytes
or `error` for a rejected peer key. Extracted pairs use `{PublicKey, SecretKey}`
order. EdDSA seeds and ECDH scalars are distinct key formats.

Use the **Hash API** when you know the output length in advance: initialize with
`hash_init/1`, add data with `hash_update/2`, and get bytes
with `hash_final/1`. `hash/2` does the same work in one call.

Use the **XOF API** when you want to read output incrementally: initialize with
`xof_init/1`, add data with `xof_absorb/2`, then call
`xof_squeeze/2` repeatedly, keeping the returned state. One-shot `xof/3` functions
return bytes. Once squeezing starts, the returned state cannot absorb more input.

Use `parallel_hash/2` or `parallel_xof/3` and their streaming counterparts to
process KT, HopMAC, and ParallelHash leaves concurrently. They produce the same
bytes as the serial APIs. Starting the application enables the router pool;
without it, parallel calls complete on the caller's dirty scheduler. The
`parallel_absorb_rounds` application option controls the worker budget, defaulting
to 100 leaves. Zero drains queued work until the pool becomes idle.

Hash and XOF states are opaque and belong to their corresponding lifecycle.
They are immutable: keep each returned state to advance; reuse an older
state to branch the input or repeat output. Lengths and ParallelHash block sizes
are measured in **bytes**, and message, key, and customization inputs accept iodata.
For TupleHash, one-shot input is a list of elements, while each update/absorb call
contributes one whole element. Element boundaries are part of the hash.

KMAC, TupleHash, and ParallelHash encode the chosen output length into their hash
results. Their XOF variants use different framing, so a shorter hash is not obtained
by truncating a longer hash or taking a prefix of the XOF. SHAKE, cSHAKE, Keccak XOF,
TurboSHAKE, KT, and HopMAC produce the same prefix through either lifecycle.
KMAC and HopMAC states contain key material and should be kept private.

The SP 800-185 functions retain the published test-vector behavior and expose
absorbing/squeezing phases described in the Revision 1 initial public draft.
See [NIST SP 800-185r1 IPD](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/185/r1/ipd) and
[RFC 9861](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9861.html).

```erlang
Digest = libdecaf:hash(sha3_256, <<"abc">>),
<<"3A985DA74FE225B2045C172D6BD390BD855F086E3E9D525B46BFE24511431532">> = binary:encode_hex(Digest).
```
""".
-moduledoc #{author => ["Andrew Bennett <potatosaladx@gmail.com>"]}.
-moduledoc #{created => "2016-02-06", modified => "2026-10-24"}.
-moduledoc #{copyright => "Andrew Bennett"}.

